---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<Base
  title="How the proof works · The Square Achievement Game"
  description="An illustrated guide to the proof that the first player wins the square achievement game exactly when n is at least 5: forcing plans, one table for every board, and the Lean formalization."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>How the proof works</h1>
    <p class="byline">An illustrated guide to the square achievement game</p>
  </header>

  <p class="abstract">
    <strong>Summary.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
    of an axis-aligned square wins. One explicit strategy, a fixed table whose plans
    stay in a 6 × 6 corner, wins every board of size at least 6, and the whole argument,
    all board sizes at once and the draws included, is machine-checked in Lean 4.
    The first player therefore has a winning strategy precisely when <span
      set:html={k("n \\ge 5")}
    />, and all smaller boards are drawn under optimal play.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="5" strategy="table"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 1. The bot moves first, replaying the strategy described below move
      for move, and you reply. Change n to explore; below 5 × 5 neither side can
      force a win.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The game</h2>
  <p>
    Played on the cells of an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, P1 moving
    first. A winning set is any
    <span
      set:html={k("\\{(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)\\}")}
    /> with <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />: the four corners of an
    axis-aligned square, from 2 × 2 blocks up. A full board with no completed
    square is a draw. Tilted squares do not count. Both players are after the
    same squares, so even a player who cannot win anymore can still block, and
    on the boards below 5 × 5 blocking is enough: neither player can force a
    win.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="A 6 by 6 grid showing the corners of a 2 by 2 block and of a square of side 3"
    >
      <defs>
        <pattern
          id="grid6"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
        >
          <path
            d="M24 0H0V24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--rule)"
            stroke-width="0.8"
            opacity="0.5"></path>
        </pattern>
      </defs>
      <rect width="144" height="144" fill="url(#grid6)"></rect>
      <rect
        width="144"
        height="144"
        fill="none"
        stroke="var(--rule)"
        stroke-width="0.8"
        opacity="0.5"></rect>
      <rect
        x="12"
        y="108"
        width="24"
        height="24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="var(--accent)"
        stroke-width="1.5"
        stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
      <rect
        x="60"
        y="12"
        width="72"
        height="72"
        fill="none"
        stroke="var(--accent)"
        stroke-width="1.5"
        stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
      <circle cx="12" cy="108" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="36" cy="108" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="12" cy="132" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="36" cy="132" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="60" cy="12" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="132" cy="12" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="60" cy="84" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      <circle cx="132" cy="84" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      Figure 2. Winning shapes: the corners of a 2 × 2 block and of a square of
      side 3. Only the corners must be held.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem 1.</span> The first player has a winning strategy
    in the square achievement game on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />.
  </div>

  <h2>History</h2>
  <p>
    Harary called games like this achievement games: both players build toward
    the same target, and the first to finish wins
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a>; the convention itself goes back to
    the positional games of Hales and Jewett
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-8">[8]</a>. Erickson asked what happens for the
    axis-aligned square on an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a>, and the question is recorded on the
    Open Problem Garden <a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>. Two ends of the
    answer were known. Jenrich reported computer searches giving draws on 3 × 3
    and 4 × 4 and a first-player win on 5 × 5
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>. At the other end, Bacher and Eliahou
    proved that every two-coloring of a 15 × 15 grid contains a monochromatic
    square
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a>, so no game on a board of size 15 or
    more can end in a draw; a standard strategy-stealing argument then gives the
    first player a win, though it produces no strategy. In between, from 6 × 6
    through 14 × 14, nothing was published until August 2026, when Keith Adler
    announced computer searches for those nine sizes
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-9">[9]</a>: a depth-limited search reports a
    first-player win within 13 plies for each of them, with full strategy trees
    certified for 6 × 6 and 7 × 7 and search verdicts for the rest, one search
    per size and no strategy shared between sizes; the results are unrefereed by
    his own account. Independently and at the same time, this work took a
    different route: one strategy for every board, machine-checked for all sizes
    at once. It is also a strategy you can watch: the bot in Figure 1 is playing
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>The strategy</h2>
  <p>
    On every turn the strategy checks two reflexes before anything else:
    complete one of your own squares if you can; otherwise, if the opponent is
    one cell from completing theirs, block it. What remains is choosing the
    quiet moves, and those come from a fixed lookup table. The strategy opens at
    (2,2), answers the opponent's first reply with a tabled second move, and the
    first two replies together select a plan: a short list of cells played in
    order. Against a first reply at (0,0) and a second at (4,4), for instance,
    the stored plan is (2,1), (1,1), (1,0), (2,3), (0,3), (2,5), (4,3). The demo
    button under the board plays full lines out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plans are forcing. Each plan move puts three corners of some square in
    place, so the opponent must block at once or lose, until one move creates
    two threats and no single block answers both. The opponent's forced blocks
    pile up too, and sometimes complete three corners of their own square; the
    block reflex answers those counter-threats, and the search that generated
    the table only accepted plans that keep the initiative through such
    exchanges.
  </p>
  <p>
    One table serves every board from 6 × 6 up, for two reasons. First, its
    plans never leave the 6 × 6 corner region of the board, so every square the
    strategy builds exists on every board containing that region. Second, a
    reply far from the corner region can join a square through two of the
    strategy's stones only from the region's own rows and columns, so far
    replies are looked up by geometric class rather than by exact position. And
    a pair of far replies can combine against a single cell of the corner
    region, three corners of a square that is invisible until that cell falls;
    we call such squares latent, and such pairs get entries of their own, keyed
    by that cell and built to never hand it over. The 5 × 5 board is the odd one
    out: it is too cramped for pure forcing, and exhaustive analysis shows that
    no all-forcing table wins there. Its table comes instead from a full
    solution of the 5 × 5 game and includes quiet moves that fork on the
    opponent's reply.
  </p>

  <h2>Why believe a computer search</h2>
  <p>
    For each board size the proof is an exhaustive search: the strategy plays
    against every sequence of replies, which at 9 × 9 means more than three
    million positions. Searches of this kind have gone wrong before, in this
    very game. The first published search for 5 × 5 contained an error and had
    to be corrected <a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We therefore wrote the checker three times, independently: once in Python,
    used while discovering the tables; once in Lean; and once in TypeScript,
    driving the bot on this page. The three replay the same exported tables and
    agree on every board size we have tried, which is reassuring but is not the
    proof. The proof rests on the Lean version alone. It comes with a soundness
    theorem, itself machine-checked, which says that whenever the checker
    answers true, a winning strategy tree exists for the game as defined
    mathematically. That definition, a few dozen lines of Lean, is the only part
    a skeptic has to read; everything else is proved against it. Nothing about
    how the tables were found has to be taken on faith; they sit in the
    development as plain data, and a wrong table would fail the check rather
    than slip through it. What remains to trust is Lean's kernel, mathlib, and
    one more thing: the exhaustive runs are discharged by <code
      >native_decide</code
    >, which has the Lean compiler evaluate the checker rather than the kernel.
    That is the standard trust base for large computations in Lean, and it is
    the whole of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Every board at once</h2>
  <p>
    Checking boards one at a time can never finish, and the hard part of the
    theorem is the tail: one argument covering every board from 17 × 17 up. The
    danger a large board adds is room. Stones placed far from the corner region
    belong to squares that exist only out there, and if one of those squares
    ever reaches three corners, the strategy must answer far from its plans.
    Figure 3 shows the regions the argument lives in, and Figure 4 a latent
    square realized against an earlier table that lacked the entries for them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The proof is a simulation. A case analysis proved in Lean shows every square
    the opponent could complete either fits inside the 17 × 17 board, where one
    exhaustive run has excluded it, or is latent, with its region cell
    determined by the two reply stones. Anchored runs check that the strategy
    never offers a latent cell as the unique block of a threat, and a replay
    argument transports the run at 17 to any board: near replies verbatim, a
    reply that ignores a threat loses to it, far replies act through stand-ins.
    The result is the theorem
    <code>check_sound_uniform</code>, whose hypotheses are finitely many
    certificates, all evaluated in seconds to minutes. With the runs at 5
    through 16, the first player wins on every board of size at least 5, with
    one explicit strategy for all sizes from 6 up.
  </p>
  <figure class="diagram">
