  <h2>The draws</h2>
  <p>
    Below 5 × 5 the first player cannot force a win: a dual search, also proved
    sound in Lean, exhibits a reply to every first-player move after which no
    win exists. That the boards are drawn, rather than lost for the first
    player, takes two more steps, both formalized. Strategy stealing shows the
    second player never forces a win on any board: a second-player strategy
    could be borrowed by the first player after an arbitrary opening move, since
    a surplus stone never hurts. Determinacy shows that a player who cannot be
    beaten can secure a draw. Together they give each player a drawing strategy
    on every board up to 4 × 4.
  </p>

  <h2>Formalization</h2>
  <p>
    The proof is formalized in Lean 4
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-6">[6]</a>, building on mathlib
    <a class="cite" href="#ref-7">[7]</a>: the game semantics, executable
    checkers with machine-checked soundness theorems, the strategy tables as
    certificates, the simulation theorem covering every board size at once, and
    the outcome theorems. The development builds with no unproved declarations.
    Its headline declarations are <code>p1_wins_iff</code>, the first player
    forces a win exactly when
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and <code>outcome</code>, which adds that
    every board up to 4 × 4 is drawn with each player able to secure at least a
    draw. Both artifacts are built from this repository on every deploy:
    <a href={`${base}downloads/threshold.pdf`}>the paper (PDF)</a> and
    <a href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
      >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    The strategy is explicit but not small: 237 branches and some eight thousand
    plans. Is there a description of a winning strategy for every board from 6 ×
    6 up that fits in a reader's head, with no lookup table, the way Jenrich's
    argument does for 5 × 5? Everything in the proof says the winning mechanism
    is local, but nobody has written it down that way.
  </p>
  <p>
    The strategy is not the fastest. Its longest verified lines run 26 plies on
    the 6 × 6, 7 × 7, and 8 × 8 boards, while Adler's searches report a forced
    win within 13 plies on each board from 6 to 14, one search per size. Is
    there one strategy that wins within 13 plies on every board from 6 × 6 up?
    More generally, how quickly can the first player force a win on the n × n
    board, and can a single strategy be fastest for every n?
  </p>
  <p>
    Are the constants least? The plans cannot fit a smaller corner than 6 × 6
    for a purely forcing table of this shape, and 17 is the smallest base board
    for the case analysis used here; whether a finer treatment of far replies
    lowers it is open.
  </p>

